{{!-- HOMEFRONT MOVES — its own tab, reached from the rail. It used to be a permanently
      visible sidebar down the right of every steading tab; the icon rail took that edge, and
      a page gives the moves room to sit two-up instead of wrapping in a narrow column.

      The Roll Modifier came across with them. It only ever governs these rolls, so it belongs
      on the same page rather than pinned beside tabs that never roll anything. It is no longer
      a SECTION of its own, though: a heading and a page-wide underline gave a three-way toggle
      the visual weight of the page's actual subject, and its stacked radio list was a leftover
      of the narrow sidebar. It rides in the moves heading now, as the same segmented pill in
      the same spot the Relationships card's table/board toggle uses.

      It is drawn only when the mode is NOT asked per roll: with "Ask How to Roll Each Time" on
      the choice moves into the pre-roll window (module/dialogs/RollDialog.js), which starts at
      Normal every time, and a sticky pill beside it would be a second answer to one question.

      `.steading-moves-tab` stays on an INNER wrapper rather than on the panel itself. It
      carries `display: flex`, and an unlayered `display` on a `.tab` beats core's layered
      `.tab { display: none }` — the panel would then show on every tab at once, the trap
      documented on `.tab.stonetop-invocations` and the Notes tabs. Off the panel, this tab's
      show/hide is Foundry's alone, exactly like Overview and Residents.

      The moves themselves are the character sheet's move CARDS, not the compact one-line rows
      they were as a sidebar. Same `.stonetop-move-group` > `.items-list` > `.stonetop-item`
      shape, so they inherit the bordered card, the underlined title row and the masonry
      columns from `.tab.moves` — this panel is a `.tab.moves` too. The text a card shows was
      the hover panel's whole job, so that panel is gone from this sheet.

      The moves keep their wrapper even though they are now the tab's only section: the fold
      walk (a heading claims every FOLLOWING SIBLING until the next heading) has no other
      boundary, so a section added here later would be swallowed by this one's caret. --}}
<div class="tab moves" data-group="primary" data-tab="moves">
	<section class="sheet-tab steading-moves-tab">

	<div class="stonetop-move-group">
		{{!-- The h3 is written out rather than taken from `stonetop.section-heading` for the
		      same reason the Relationships card writes its own: the partial has no slot for a
		      control, and the pill has to sit INSIDE the heading — that is what puts it on the
		      heading's own line, above the underline, instead of in the padding above it.

		      The tab's name lives in a tooltip now that the rail is icon-only, so this heading
		      is also the only place the page says what it is. --}}
		<h3 class="stonetop-move-group-title">{{localize "stonetop.steading.tabs.moves"}}
			{{#if stonetop.showRollModeControl}}{{> "stonetop.roll-mode-picker" rollMode=stonetop.rollMode}}{{/if}}
			{{> "stonetop.section-collapse" collapse="steadingMoves"}}
		</h3>
		{{!-- WHAT A MOVE DOES WHEN CLICKED rides on the `<li>`, not on a control inside it.

		      There is no dice button at the head of a move any more, in either shape. The NAME
		      was always the bigger target and always did exactly the same thing — the icon's
		      click handler is the one the name forwarded to — so the icon was a second door onto
		      one room, and the row reads as a title rather than a toolbar without it.

		      That leaves the row's two clickable parts (the name and the "+STAT" chip) with
		      nowhere of their own to carry what the move IS, so the `<li>` carries it. WHICH of
		      the two it carries — a slug for a move with a walkthrough, a name and stat for one
		      that only rolls — is decided once in StonetopSteadingSheet#getData and arrives here
		      already resolved, so this template and the classic row cannot disagree about what a
		      click means. Both openers reach the `<li>` with `closest("li")` — the same hop the
		      name-click already made to find the button. StonetopSteadingSheet#activateListeners
		      reads these; renaming one silently stops that move working. --}}
		<ol class="items-list">
			{{#each stonetop.moves}}
			<li class="stonetop-item steading-move-card{{#if unowned}} move-unowned{{/if}}"
			    data-move-slug="{{moveSlug}}" data-move-name="{{moveName}}" data-stat="{{stat}}">
				{{!-- The "+STAT" chip sits AFTER the name, which has `flex: 1` — so it rides the
				      far right of the card's header line rather than crowding in front of the
				      title. Same placement the character sheet's Other Moves list uses. It stays
				      a click target: tapping the stat rolls the move, as tapping the name does. --}}
				<div class="stonetop-item-header">
					<strong class="stonetop-item-name stonetop-steading-move-open">{{label}}</strong>
					{{#if (and @root.stonetop.showRollStatChips statChipLabel)}}
					<span class="stonetop-move-roll-chip steading-move-stat-tag">+{{statChipLabel}}</span>
					{{/if}}
				</div>
				{{#if description}}<div class="stonetop-item-description">{{{moveBody description moveResults}}}</div>{{/if}}
			</li>
			{{/each}}
		</ol>
	</div>

	</section>
</div>
